  "result": "Blocked by robots.txt\nThe following domains cannot be accessed (non-retryable error): hadiasghari.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03315)\nciteturn8895academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons ... Date: Wed Oct  1 00:29:39 2025\nTitle: Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\nAuthors: Alex Gibson\nDate: Wed Oct  1 00:29:39 2025\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn't activate on the calibration text.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political (https://hadiasghari.com/mirror/AsghariNenno2024_FramesInLLMs_AIMLAI24b.pdf)\nciteturn8895search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Mechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political ... Inspired by mechanistic interpretability research, ... Almost every word in language evokes a frame, although the evoked mental\nMechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political\nFrames in Language Models: an Exploration\nHadi Asghari1,2(\u0000) and Sami Nenno1\n1 Humboldt Institute for Internet & Society, Berlin, Germany\n2 Technische Universität Berlin, Berlin, Germany\nhadi.asghari@hiig.de\nAbstract. This paper explores the ability of large language models\nto generate and recognize deep cognitive frames, particularly in socio-\npolitical contexts. We demonstrate that LLMs are highly fluent in gen-\nerating texts that evoke specific frames and can recognize these frames\nin zero-shot settings. Inspired by mechanistic interpretability research,\nwe investigate the location of the ‘strict father’ and ‘nurturing parent’\nframes within the model’s hidden representation, identifying singular di-\nmensions that correlate strongly with their presence. Our findings con-\ntribute to understanding how LLMs capture and express meaningful hu-\nman concepts.\nKeywords: Cognitive Frames · LLMs · Interpretability.\n1\nIntroduction\nThe question of how large language models (LLMs)–despite being compared\nto “stochastic parrots” [4] or even “autocomplete on steroids”–can converse with\nhumans in ways that appear meaningful and seem to show understanding is quite\nintriguing. One possible explanation is that their internal representation must be\nable to capture meaningful concepts for humans. In this paper, we focus on one\nset of such meaningful and critical concepts, cognitive frames, and investigate\nthe fluency of LLMs in generating and recognizing them.\nFrames are “mental structures that shape the way we see the world” [20].\nAlmost every word in language evokes a frame, although the evoked mental\nstructures can be at different levels of abstraction. Of particular interest to this\npaper are cognitive frames that underlie socio-political discourses. Famous ex-\namples of such frames include the ‘strict father’ (SF) and ‘nurturing parent’\n(NP), two parenting and family models that different parts of society value dif-\nferently. Importantly, people’s beliefs about the morality of these parenting styles\ninform their views on many socio-political issues, due to viewing “the nation as\nfamily” [19].\nBased on this motivation, we set out to answer two research questions. The\nfirst is how well current LLMs “understand” socio-political frames–in the sense\nof how fluently they can generate texts that evoke them, and how well can\nthey recognize their implicit presence in texts. We will show in later sections--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=OIcNvCOpoB)\nciteturn8895search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons ... • **A decomposition of first-layer attention into positional and content-dependent components.\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson  \nUniversity of Cambridge  \nag2299@cam.ac.uk\n\n**Abstract**\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn’t activate on the calibration text.\n\n**1  Introduction:**\n\nTransformer language models have achieved remarkable capabilities, yet our mechanistic understanding of how they represent text remains limited. Prior work such as the Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits (Elhage et al., 2021) has helped to reveal components of models which implement specific algorithmic behaviors, such as induction heads (Olsson et al., 2022), or duplicate token heads (Wang et al., 2023). These results illustrate that in some cases attention heads implement clear symbolic algorithms.\n\nBuilding on these insights into token-level pattern recognition, a natural next question concerns how transformers represent higher-level, distributed properties of text-such as writing style, domain, or linguistic variety-that emerge from broader contextual patterns rather than individual tokens. While existing approaches like Sparse Autoencoders (Bricken et al., 2023) and probing methods (Gurnee et al., 2023; Bills et al., 2023) have successfully identified many such contextual features, developing a more mechanistic understanding of how these representations arise from the weights of a transformer remains a challenge.\n\nIn this paper, we take a small step towards improving said mechanistic understanding, using GPT-2 Small as a case study. Our contributions are:\n\n• **A decomposition of first-layer attention into positional and content-dependent components.** We approximate each head by a positional kernel-its baseline positional preference if tokens were identical-together with token-dependent adjustments.\n\n• **Identification of stable heads.** We argue, and support empirically, that heads with broad positional kernels and weak content dependence often have stable softmax denominators that concentrate around their mean for the current distribution of tokens.\n\n• **The contextual circuit approximation.** By sampling softmax denominators from a representative \"calibration text\", we can combine several such stable heads in GPT-2 Small, and construct what we call the contextual circuit: a linear summary of the surrounding text that approximates how these heads collectively influence downstream neurons.\n\nMechanistic Interpretability Workshop at NeurIPS 2025.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch Publications | Hadi Asghari (https://hadiasghari.com/publications.html)\nciteturn8895search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Mechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political Frames in Language Models  | H. ... AI for the Public: How Public Interest Theory Shifts the Discourse on AI  | T. ... Collectively Exercising the Right of Access: Individual Effort, Societal Effect  | R. ... Cybersecurity via Intermediaries: Analyzing Security Measurements to Understand Intermediary Incentives and Inform Public Policy _{(PDF)}  | H. ... Mueller  | Telecommunications Research Policy Conference, 2013  | ... Mueller  | Global Internet Governance Academic Network, 2012  | ... The Role of ISPs in Botnet Mitigation: An Empirical Analysis Based on Spam Data  | M. van Eeten, J.\n\n# Research Publications\n\nTitle  | Authors  | In, Year  | S.Media\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nGenerative AI and the Future of the Digital Commons  | A. Noroozian, L. Aldana, M. Arisi, H. Asghari, R. Avila, …  | CACM, 2026  |  \nMany LLMs Are More Utilitarian Than One  | A. Keshmirian, R. Baltaji, B. Hemmatian, H. Asghari, L. Varshney  | NeurIPS, 2025  | post\nTriangles, Justice, and AI: Testing LLMs’ Comprehension of Political Ideologies  | H. Asghari & F. Bialy  | Philosophy & Digitality, 2025  |  \nProceedings of the 2nd Workshop on Public Interest AI collocated with KI2024  | H. Asghari & T. Züger (Eds.)  | CEUR-WS, 2025   |  \nMechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political Frames in Language Models  | H. Asghari & S. Nenno  | ECML AIMLAI, 2024  |  \nAI Systems for the Public Interest (Editorial)  | T. Züger & H. Asghari  | Internet Policy Review, 2024  | post\nElaborative Simplification for German-language Texts  | F. Hewett, H. Asghari, M. Stede  | SIGDIAL, 2024  |  \nEs gibt keinen Algorithmus gegen Hass  | T. Züger & H. Asghari  | Code & Vorurteil, 2024  |  \nOn the Prevalence of Leichte Sprache on the German Web  | H. Asghari, F. Hewett, & T. Züger  | ACM WebSci, 2023  | blog\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/decomposing-attention-to-find-context-sensitive-neurons)\nciteturn8895search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago;   * Restricted Architectures: The analysis focuses on layer 0 of models using additive absolute positional embeddings (and conceptually ALiBi/T5). ...   * Future Directions: Refining calibration by clustering texts prior to denominator estimation, extending the decomposition to deeper network layers, and formulating approximations adapted to RoPE. ...       title = {{Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons}},\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\n  * Restricted Architectures: The analysis focuses on layer 0 of models using additive absolute positional embeddings (and conceptually ALiBi/T5). Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE, e.g., Pythia) break translation equivariance and position-independent key semantics, making RoPE models much harder to analyze.\n  * Stop-Word Distribution Shifts: The denominator approximation degrades when the underlying token distribution shifts radically (e.g., analyzing programming code using a prose-derived calibration text).\n  * Systematic Bias: The linear contextual circuit exhibits a small unexplained systematic offset in pre-activation values compared to ground truth.\n  * Future Directions: Refining calibration by clustering texts prior to denominator estimation, extending the decomposition to deeper network layers, and formulating approximations adapted to RoPE.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @inproceedings{gibson2025decomposing,\n      author = {Alex Gibson},\n      title = {{Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons}},\n      booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems},\n      year = {2025},\n      url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=OIcNvCOpoB},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2510.03315v1)\nciteturn8895search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons ... A crucial insight enabling this analysis is that certain attention heads exhibit stable softmax denominators. ... Building on attention decomposition and denominator stability, the research constructs a \"contextual circuit\" that approximates how selected attention heads (specifically heads 0, 2, 6, 8, 9, and 10) collectively contribute to downstream neuron activations. ... @misc{gibson2025decomposingattentioncontextsensitiveneurons, title={Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons}, author={Alex Gibson}, year={2025}, eprint={2510.03315}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CL}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03315}, }\nSubmitted 01 Oct 2025\n\nen\n\n# Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAG Alex Gibson\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn't activate on the calibration text.\n\n## Understanding Attention Decomposition in Language Models\n\nImage: Attention Decomposition Accuracy\n## Citation\n\nC