tive Neurons ... We study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content.\n\n# Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\n  * October 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2510.03315\n\nAuthors:\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nAlex Gibson\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn't activate on the calibration text.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (0)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any references for this publication.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Gibson_A in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AGibson_A+in%3Acs)\nciteturn8895search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\n8 results for au:Gibson_A in:cs\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nOct 07 2025 cs.CL cs.AI cs.LG arXiv:2510.03315v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.03315, author = {Alex Gibson}, title = {{D}ecomposing {A}ttention {T}o {F}ind {C}ontext-{S}ensitive {N}eurons}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.03315}, note = {arXiv:2510.03315v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn't activate on the calibration text.\n\n  * Invisible Labor: The Backbone of Open Source Software\n\nRobin A. Lange, Anna Gibson, Milo Z. Trujillo, Brooke Foucault Welles\n\nMar 19 2025 cs.SI cs.SE arXiv:2503.13405v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2503.13405, author = {Robin A.~Lange and Anna Gibson and Milo Z.~Trujillo and Brooke Foucault Welles}, title = {{I}nvisible {L}abor: {T}he {B}ackbone of {O}pen {S}ource {S}oftware}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2503.13405}, note = {arXiv:2503.13405v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-13&page=126&range=7)\nciteturn8895search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\nHowever, most EOFM models are trained only on single modalities of data and then applied or benchmarked by matching bands across different modalities. It is not clear from existing work what impact diverse sensor architectures have on the internal representations of the present suite of EOFMs. We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.\n\n  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nOct 07 2025 cs.CL cs.AI cs.LG arXiv:2510.03315v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.03315, author = {Alex Gibson}, title = {{D}ecomposing {A}ttention {T}o {F}ind {C}ontext-{S}ensitive {N}eurons}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.03315}, note = {arXiv:2510.03315v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/decomposing-attention-to-find-context-sensitive-neurons/wrk_8790d460d6eb99670cd245b8916b8846)\nciteturn8895search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons ... Using GPT-2 Small as a primary case study, the authors introduce a weight-driven analysis pipeline: ... Context-Sensitive Neuron Discovery: Downstream MLP neurons with large incoming weights from this contextual circuit are extracted. ...   * Future Directions: Refining calibration by clustering texts prior to denominator estimation, extending the decomposition to deeper network layers, and formulating approximations adapted to RoPE.\n\n# Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nNeurIPS 2025 · OpenReview\n\nDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neuronsneurips · 2025PresentationDecomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neuronsarxiv · 2025\n\n## TL;DR\n\nBy decomposing first-layer transformer attention into decoupled positional and content terms, this paper demonstrates that attention heads with broad, diffuse positional patterns exhibit remarkably stable softmax denominators. By approximating these denominators using just a single \"calibration text,\" the collective output of these heads simplifies into a linear bag-of-words summary, enabling researchers to discover hundreds of high-level context-sensitive neurons directly from model weights without scanning a large corpus.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nMechanistic interpretability has succeeded at reverse-engineering token-level algorithms (e.g., induction heads and duplicate token heads). However, language models also track higher-level distributed contextual properties—such as dialect, genre, domain, or tone.\n\nCurrently, researchers find such features using black-box techniques like probing or Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs), which require running models over massive text datasets and are prone to dataset-specific artifacts (\"interpretability illusions\"). This paper investigates how high-level context representations are constructed mechanistically directly from the model weights of early transformer layers.\n\n## Methods\n\nUsing GPT-2 Small as a primary case study, the authors introduce a weight-driven analysis pipeline:\n\n  1. Decoupled Key Approximation: Because LayerNorm nonlinearly entangles token embeddings ($W_E$) and positional embeddings ($W_{pos}$), the authors approximate keys by linearizing the LayerNorm denominator around the attending position $n$. This splits attention keys into independent content ($E^h[n, x_i]$) and position ($P^h[n, i]$) terms.\n  2. Positional Kernels & Factored Attention: Attention probabilities decompose into the product of an intrinsic positional kernel and a content-dependent term: $$\\text{attn\\_approx}^h[n, i] \\propto \\text{pos}^h_{n,i,x_n} \\cdot \\text{content}^h_{n,x_i,x_n}$$\n  3. Softmax Denominator Stability via Concentration of Measure: When attention heads have broad positional kernels and weak content dependence, their positionally normalized softmax denominators concentrate around their expectation (governed by Hoeffding and Chebyshev bounds proportional to $\\sum \\text{pos}_i^2$).\n  4. The Contextual Circuit: Combining 6 \"slowly decaying\" heads ($H = \\{0, 2, 6, 8, 9, 10\\}$) in layer 0, the authors fix their softmax denominators using a single representative calibration text and share a median positional kernel. The multi-head Output-Value (OV) projection collapses into a simple linear, position-weighted token summary entering the layer-0 MLP.\n  5. Context-Sensitive Neuron Discovery: Downstream MLP neurons with large incoming weights from this contextual circuit are extracted. Their top positive and negative token contributions function as Naive Bayes classifiers for contextual attributes.\n\n## Datasets\n\n  * OpenWebText: Used to evaluate Total Variation (TV) distances between true and approximate attention patterns, observe denominator stability across 1,000 texts, and benchmark neuron activation predictions.\n  * Model checkpoints evaluated: GPT-2 Small (primary focus), Pythia, and TinyStories.\n  * DeepSeek-R1: Employed as an auxiliary tool to summarize and semantically label the top-50 token contributions of discovered neurons.\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * Broad Attention Heads Act as Linear Spatial Filters: While softmax attention is strictly nonlinear, heads that cast wide, diffuse attention effectively act as smooth moving averages over text because their softmax denominators barely fluctuate across typical prose.\n  * Weights-Only Interpretability: Instead of identifying neurons via expensive activations over millions of tokens, high-level neurons (such as Neuron 704, which discriminates Commonwealth vs. American English) can be found strictly by inspecting weight projections from the contextual circuit.\n  * Immunity to Corpus Bias: Neurons identified by this method do not even need to activate on the calibration text itself, eliminating dataset artifacts and illusions.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Attention Approximation Fidelity: The total variation (TV) distance between true attention patterns and the factored key approximation was typically $\\sim 0.05$ (meaning only 5% of attention mass was displaced).\n  * Denominator Concentration: Across 1,000 random OpenWebText samples, denominators for broad heads clustered closely around a stable central value, only showing outliers on text with highly abnormal stop-word frequencies (e.g., source code).\n  * Predictive Accuracy: Pre-activations predicted by the linear contextual circuit were strongly correlated with true model pre-activations across 1,000 OpenWebText samples:\n  * Median Pearson correlation ($r$): $0.9485$\n  * Median Fraction of Variance Unexplained (FVU): $0.1426$\n  * Neuron Discoveries: The authors cataloged over 100 contextual neurons in GPT-2 Small, identifying dedicated units for programming languages, legal proceedings, US politics, biology, philosophy, and creative writing.\n  * Minimal Compute: All experiments required less than 10 minutes total on a single Google Colab T4 GPU (16 GB).\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\n  * Restricted Architectures: The analysis focuses on layer 0 of models using additive absolute positional embeddings (and conceptually ALiBi/T5). Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE, e.g., Pythia) break translation equivariance and position-independent key semantics, making RoPE models much harder to analyze.\n  * Stop-Word Distribution Shifts: The denominator approximation degrades when the underlying token distribution shifts radically (e.g., analyzing programming code using a prose-derived calibration text).\n  * Systematic Bias: The linear contextual circuit exhibits a small unexplained systematic offset in pre-activation values compared to ground truth.\n  * Future Directions: Refining calibration by clustering texts prior to denominator estimation, extending the decomposition to deeper network layers, and formulating approximations adapted to RoPE.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-10&page=107&range=7)\nciteturn8895search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\nHowever, most EOFM models are trained only on single modalities of data and then applied or benchmarked by matching bands across different modalities. It is not clear from existing work what impact diverse sensor architectures have on the internal representations of the present suite of EOFMs. We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.\n\n  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nOct 07 2025 cs.CL cs.AI cs.LG arXiv:2510.03315v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.03315, author = {Alex Gibson}, title = {{D}ecomposing {A}ttention {T}o {F}ind {C}ontext-{S}ensitive {N}eurons}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.03315}, note = {arXiv:2510.03315v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-07&page=37&range=3)\nciteturn8895search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\nHowever, most EOFM models are trained only on single modalities of data and then applied or benchmarked by matching bands across different modalities. It is not clear from existing work what impact diverse sensor architectures have on the internal representations of the present suite of EOFMs. We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.\n\n  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nOct 07 2025 cs.CL cs.AI cs.LG arXiv:2510.03315v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.03315, author = {Alex Gibson}, title = {{D}ecomposing {A}ttention {T}o {F}ind {C}ontext-{S}ensitive {N}eurons}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.03315}, note = {arXiv:2510.03315v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-07&page=55&range=31)\nciteturn8895search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons ... We study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content.\nWe show in this work that the representation space of EOFMs is highly sensitive to sensor architecture and that understanding this difference gives a vital perspective on the pitfalls of current EOFM design and signals for how to move forward as model developers, users, and a community guided by robust remote-sensing science.\n\n  * Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons\n\nAlex Gibson\n\nOct 07 2025 cs.CL cs.AI cs.LG arXiv:2510.03315v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.03315, author = {Alex Gibson}, title = {{D}ecomposing {A}ttention {T}o {F}ind {C}ontext-{S}ensitive {N}eurons}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.03315}, note = {arXiv:2510.03315v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. "
